# Classical and Folk Dances of India: Eight Classical Forms, Dozens of Folk

> India's dance map has two layers: the eight classical forms
> recognised by the Sangeet Natak Akademi, each bound to a state and a
> scripture-rooted grammar, and beneath them a living sea of folk
> dances tied to harvests, weddings, and festivals. The exam asks
> both layers, and the trap is always the same — a classical form
> offered as folk, or the wrong state attached to a famous name.

## 1. The Eight Classical Dances

[definition]
India recognises **eight classical dance forms**, all tracing their
grammar to the **Natyashastra** of **Bharata Muni** (c. 200 BCE–200
CE), the ancient treatise on dramaturgy. The eight: Bharatanatyam,
Kathak, Kathakali, Mohiniyattam, Kuchipudi, Odissi, Manipuri, and —
recognised most recently in 2000 — **Sattriya**.
[/definition]

| Form | State | Signature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bharatanatyam | Tamil Nadu | Temple tradition; fixed upper-body geometry, adavus (steps), from the Tanjore quartet's codification |
| Kathak | Uttar Pradesh / North India | Storytelling dance of the Mughal courts; footwork (tatkar), spins (chakkars); the Jaipur, Lucknow, and Banaras gharanas |
| Kathakali | Kerala | Dance-drama of the epics; elaborate painted face (pacha-green for noble heroes, kathi for villains); all-male traditionally |
| Mohiniyattam | Kerala | "Dance of the enchantress"; lasya (graceful) style, performed by women, swaying palm-leaf movement |
| Kuchipudi | Andhra Pradesh | Originally all-male Bhagavata Mela tradition from Siddhendra Yogi; famous tarangam — dancing on a brass plate's rim |
| Odissi | Odisha | Oldest by sculptural evidence; tribhanga (three-bend) posture; Mahari temple dancers |
| Manipuri | Manipur | Ras Leela of Krishna devotion; rounded, soft movements; dancers do not wear ankle bells |
| Sattriya | Assam | Created by the Vaishnavite saint Sankardev in the sattra monasteries; recognised as classical in 2000 |

Two clues that recur in question papers: Manipuri's **no ankle
bells** rule (every other classical form's percussion includes
ghungroos), and Odissi's **tribhanga** three-bends posture mirrored
in the temple sculptures of Konark and Udayagiri. Kathak's Mughal
court history is what separates it from the temple-born southern
forms — the examiner's "which classical dance developed in the
Mughal courts" is answered *Kathak*.

## 2. The Exponent Ladder — Dancers to Know

| Dancer | Form |
| --- | --- |
| Rukmini Devi Arundale | Bharatanatyam (reviver; Kalakshetra founder) |
| Yamini Krishnamurthy, Leela Samson | Bharatanatyam |
| Birju Maharaj, Sitara Devi, Uma Sharma | Kathak |
| Kelucharan Mohapatra, Sonal Mansingh | Odissi |
| Kalamandalam Gopi (and the Kalamandalam lineage) | Kathakali |
| Vempati Chinna Satyam, Raja-Radha Reddy | Kuchipudi |
| Guru Bipin Singh, Jhaveri sisters (Nayana, Suverna...) | Manipuri |
| Rukmini Devi, Mrinalini Sarabhai (multi-form) | — |

Rukmini Devi's revival of Bharatanatyam in the 1930s — rescuing it
from Victorian-era social stigma and founding **Kalakshetra** at
Chennai — is the standard history MCQ. Birju Maharaj (Kathaks of the
Lucknow gharana) and Kelucharan Mohapatra (Odissi's 20th-century
architect) complete the trio most often quoted.

## 3. The Folk Layer — Regional Dances and Their Festivals

| Dance | State | Occasion / note |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bhangra, Giddha | Punjab | Harvest (Baisakhi); Giddha is the women's form |
| Garba, Dandiya Raas | Gujarat | Navratri; Dandiya uses sticks in pairs |
| Ghoomar, Kalbelia | Rajasthan | Kalbelia is UNESCO-listed intangible heritage |
| Bihu | Assam | Spring (Bohag Bihu) festival |
| Lavani | Maharashtra | Rhythmic song-dance of the Tamasha tradition |
| Chhau | Odisha / Jharkhand / West Bengal | Masked dance; UNESCO intangible heritage list |
| Nati | Himachal Pradesh | Kullu Dussehra; several regional variants |
| Rouf | Jammu & Kashmir (Kashmir Valley) | Spring, women's dance |
| Yakshagana | Karnataka | All-night dance-drama of the epics |
| Theyyam | Kerala | Ritual possession-performance, north Malabar |
| Dekhni | Goa | Song-dance of the Konkan coast |
| Karma, Panthi | Chhattisgarh | Panthi by the Satnami community |
| Jat-Jatin, Jhijhia | Bihar | Jhijhia is performed for rain and protection |
| Dhol Cholom | Manipur | Holi drum dance |
| Kummi, Karagattam | Tamil Nadu | Karagattam balances a pot on the head |

The two **UNESCO intangible cultural heritage** entries on the folk
side — **Kalbelia** (Rajasthan) and **Chhau** (the eastern masked
dance) — are modern paper favourites, precisely because the
"classical vs folk" boundary is where candidates slip.

## 4. Reading a Dance Question Without Panic

Dance questions resolve by elimination along three axes: **state**
(a Kerala question with "Odissi" as an option is solved by state),
**style** (court/temple/monastery/village), and **props** (ankle
bells, masks, plates, sticks, pots). A question that mentions
"dancing on the rim of a brass plate" is Kuchipudi regardless of
which states crowd the options; "masked dance from eastern India"
is Chhau; "no ghungroos" points to Manipuri; "spins and footwork of
the Mughal court" to Kathak; "enchantress of Kerala" to Mohiniyattam.

Finally, remember that the classification belongs to the **Sangeet
Natak Akademi**, India's national academy of music, dance, and drama
(founded 1952, an autonomous body under the Ministry of Culture) —
"which body recognises the classical dances" is a meta-question that
appears once every few years, and the trap option is the Lalit Kala
Akademi, which is the *visual* arts academy.

[example]
A paper asks: "Which classical dance of India is traditionally
performed without ankle bells (ghungroos)?" — options: Kathak,
Bharatanatyam, Manipuri, Kathakali. The answer is Manipuri, whose
soft, rounded aesthetic treats percussion as external (the pung
drum) rather than worn. The follow-up inverts the clue: "which form
is famous for fast chakkars (spins)?" — Kathak, with Manipuri now
as the decoy. One prop fact answered two questions.
[/example]

[summary]
The eight classical forms with their states: Bharatanatyam (Tamil
Nadu), Kathak (UP, Mughal courts, chakkars), Kathakali (Kerala,
painted faces), Mohiniyattam (Kerala, women's lasya), Kuchipudi
(Andhra, tarangam plate), Odissi (Odisha, tribhanga), Manipuri (no
ankle bells, Ras Leela), Sattriya (Assam, Sankardev, recognised
2000). All descend from Bharata's Natyashastra; recognition rests
with the Sangeet Natak Akademi. Folk map: Bhangra-Giddha (Punjab),
Garba-Dandiya (Gujarat), Ghoomar and the UNESCO-listed Kalbelia
(Rajasthan), Bihu (Assam), Lavani (Maharashtra), UNESCO-listed
Chhau (eastern India), Yakshagana (Karnataka), Theyyam (Kerala),
Nati (Himachal). Decode questions by state, style, and prop.
[/summary]
